<BaseLayout title="Operations Management & People Leadership" current="/operations-leadership/" description="Richard Allen's operations-management and people-leadership experience across SaaS support, workforce operations, project coordination, reporting, process improvement, coaching, and warehouse management.">
<section class="page-intro"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">Operations Management & People Leadership</div><h1>Build structure around people, priorities, workflows, data, and change.</h1><p class="lede">My management experience combines direct people leadership with the operating work behind it: allocating work, maintaining coverage, setting and monitoring performance measures, coaching employees, handling corrective action, managing projects, improving procedures, presenting operational reporting, and coordinating across functions.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="card"><h3>Managing a 32-person support organization</h3><p>Progressed from Team Lead to Customer Support Manager at HawkSoft in 2015 and managed 32 Customer Support and Technical Support representatives. I held hiring, firing, and disciplinary authority and managed attendance, workload coverage, workflow delegation, escalations, quality, performance evaluations, coaching, and staff development. As a manager I worked alongside three peer managers.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>Operational goals, cadence & performance</h3><p>Helped establish staff and departmental objectives and key results, with measures including calls taken, calls resolved, calls forwarded to Tier 2/3, call length, support tickets taken, tickets closed, and other service metrics. My team frequently exceeded peer teams on company OKRs. I also led recurring stand-ups, check-ins, planning, strategy, review, and tactical meetings.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>Management reporting & executive decisions</h3><p>Analyzed raw customer-service and phone data, built recurring reports, identified trends, and made recommendations on staffing, training, policy, procedures, and future needs. Early on my manager presented the reporting; I later presented most of it myself to directors and upper management, and the findings drove procedure additions, removals, and other course corrections.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>Cross-functional management</h3><p>Managed multiple small-to-medium projects, coordinated software and database migrations, and regularly attended and contributed to Product Reviews and third-party partnership meetings—sometimes presenting or leading. My work repeatedly connected support, development, sales, customer experience, and leadership.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>Workforce operations at scale</h3><p>As Position Manager / Operations & Policy Analyst II at Oregon Health Authority, managed records and tracking for 400+ positions, maintained organizational charts and position trackers, produced 20+ recurring and specialized reports, and coordinated with Recruiting, HR, Classification & Compensation, Budget, DEI, and leadership. Reporting included monthly and specialized work for the CFO area and, at times, the CFO’s superior.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>Reporting in administrative operations</h3><p>At Marion County Health & Human Services, coordinated scheduling, intake, service, and administrative workflows while maintaining operational and EHR reporting. As with my earlier management reporting, my supervisor initially presented my work; I later personally presented most reports and findings to management.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>Process governance</h3><p>At Oregon Employment Department, led a year-long SOP project and project team that created 100 new SOPs, corrected and standardized 25+ legacy SOPs, established standards, built a centralized repository, reviewed completed work, coached contributors, and set ongoing maintenance guidelines.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3>Warehouse & supply operations</h3><p>As Aramark Warehouse Manager from 2001–2003, I typically supervised about 20 people—approximately three warehouse workers, seven drivers, and at least ten inmate workers. I handled purchasing and supply-management duties, receiving, storage and distribution, shipping, sanitation, warehouse operations, staff training, and fleet/driver coordination, with more than $1 million in inventory annually.</p></article>
